@page "/Home"

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Earth Vertical Datum</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice performs stateless depth conversions in both directions between the EGM84 mean-sea-level geoid and the WGS84 reference ellipsoid.
    Public latitude and longitude values are WGS84 SI radians. Input and output depths are SI metres, positive downward,
    and are explicitly named for their reference surfaces. The web interface can display other units through the OSDC
    unit-reference system while preserving SI at the API boundary.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Geoid versus ellipsoid</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>reference ellipsoid</b> is a smooth mathematical surface, flattened slightly at the poles, chosen
                to approximate the overall shape of the Earth. It has a simple closed-form equation, so positions on
                or above it can be computed exactly. WGS84 is such an ellipsoid.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                A <b>geoid</b> is not a mathematical shape but a physical one: the equipotential surface of the
                Earth's gravity field that best coincides with global mean sea level. Because the Earth's mass is
                unevenly distributed, the geoid undulates a few tens of metres above and below the reference
                ellipsoid, depending on location. That local separation is the <b>geoid undulation</b> (also called
                geoid height): positive where the geoid sits above the ellipsoid, negative where it sits below.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Mean sea level (EGM84)</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Depths measured from "mean sea level" are, in practice, depths measured from a gravimetric geoid
                model: a surface fitted to the long-term average sea surface after removing tides, waves, and
                currents. This service uses <b>EGM84</b>, an Earth Gravitational Model published by the U.S. National
                Geospatial-Intelligence Agency, sampled here on a 30 arc-minute grid distributed with GeographicLib.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The loaded grid is interpolated cubically to give a smooth undulation value at any latitude and
                longitude, together with its published maximum and RMS interpolation error. Exact model identity,
                accuracy figures, and a SHA-256 of the coefficient file are available on the
                <MudLink Href="/EarthVerticalDatum/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumModel">Geoid model</MudLink> page and via
                the model-information API and MCP tool.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">WGS84</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                The <b>World Geodetic System 1984 (WGS84)</b> is the geodetic reference frame used by GPS and almost
                every other GNSS. It defines an origin and orientation fixed to the Earth's centre of mass and crust,
                and a reference ellipsoid with a semi-major axis of 6,378,137 m and a flattening of 1/298.257223563.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                A GNSS receiver naturally reports height (or, here, depth) relative to this ellipsoid, not relative
                to mean sea level. Latitude and longitude in this service are always WGS84 geodetic coordinates,
                expressed in SI radians.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why this matters</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Depths surveyed with GNSS equipment are naturally ellipsoidal, while depths reported against
                traditional chart datums, drilling references, or bathymetry are naturally orthometric — referenced
                to mean sea level. Mixing the two without converting introduces an error equal to the local geoid
                undulation, which can reach several tens of metres.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                This microservice exists to remove that ambiguity: given a WGS84 position, it converts a depth from
                one reference surface to the other, deterministically and without needing a full geodesy toolkit
                in every consuming application.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">How the conversion works</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        <code>GeoidUndulation</code> is defined positive upward, so that
        <code>Wgs84EllipsoidalDepth = MeanSeaLevelDepth − GeoidUndulation</code> and, inversely,
        <code>MeanSeaLevelDepth = Wgs84EllipsoidalDepth + GeoidUndulation</code>. Both depths, by OSDC convention,
        are SI metres positive downward. The service looks up the EGM84 undulation at the supplied WGS84 latitude
        and longitude and applies it in the requested direction; GeographicLib's own degree and positive-up
        conventions are confined to the implementation and never appear at the API boundary.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Every calculation request is stateless and atomic: inputs and results are not persisted, positions in a
        batch are validated and converted together, and results preserve input order. Only aggregate usage counters
        are retained for operational statistics.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/EarthVerticalDatum/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumCalculation">Convert depth</MudLink>, choose
        a direction — mean sea level → WGS84 or WGS84 → mean sea level — enter the WGS84 latitude and longitude and
        the input depth, then press <i>Convert</i>. Latitude and longitude can be entered as sexagesimal or decimal
        degrees; the depth field and the result both follow the unit system selected for the page, while the
        service itself always exchanges SI radians and SI metres. The result panel also reports the EGM84 geoid
        undulation used for that position and the identity of the loaded model.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <MudLink Href="/EarthVerticalDatum/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumModel">Geoid model</MudLink> page shows the
        loaded grid's identity, resolution, interpolation method, published accuracy, and coefficient checksum. The
        <MudLink Href="/EarthVerticalDatum/webapp/StatisticsEarthVerticalDatum">Usage statistics</MudLink> page shows
        cumulative REST and MCP request counters. The service periodically saves them to its configured JSON file,
        which defaults to <code>/home/EarthVerticalDatum.UsageStatistics.json</code> in the container. They survive a
        restart when that directory is backed by persistent storage.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same conversion is reachable through several interfaces built on the same stateless model, so it can be
        used interactively or wired into other systems and AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for manual, one-off conversions and for
                    inspecting the loaded geoid model and usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/EarthVerticalDatum/api/EarthVerticalDatum</code> for discovery,
                    model information, and synchronous batch conversion in either direction, documented and
                    explorable through the Swagger UI at <code>/EarthVerticalDatum/api/swagger</code>. Suited to
                    scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at
                    <code>/EarthVerticalDatum/api/mcp</code>, publishing four tools — connectivity <code>ping</code>,
                    <code>earth_vertical_datum_get_model_info</code>, and the two conversion tools — with strict
                    input/output JSON Schemas, so AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can call the same conversions
                    directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the "Convert depth", "Geoid model", and "Usage statistics" pages
                    are published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.EarthVerticalDatum.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so
                    other Blazor applications can embed the same unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing
                    this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
